+<div class="faq-item__a font-ibm-mono" data-type="meta" hidden=""><p data-type="body">It’s a fair question, and the honest answer is: it depends on what you’re doing. Some projects genuinely don’t need one but most do. As a general guide, you will typically need a structural engineer for:</p>
 <p data-type="body"><strong>New builds and major extensions.</strong> Any new dwelling or significant addition to an existing one will require structural drawings and calculations as part of the documentation package. If the project involves a concrete slab, timber or steel framing, or any structural element that supports load, an engineer needs to design and document it.</p>
 <p data-type="body"><strong>Removing or modifying walls.</strong> Before any wall comes down, you need to know whether it’s structural. If it is, an engineer needs to design the beam that replaces it, specify the posts and footings, and confirm the load path through the structure. Getting this wrong is not a minor issue.</p>
 <p data-type="body"><strong>Alterations to existing structures.</strong> Extending a deck, adding a storey, opening up a façade, or inserting large glazed openings all require engineering input. The existing structure was designed for a specific set of loads and conditions. Changing it — even in ways that seem minor — can have consequences that aren’t visible until something moves.</p>
